<worksheet>
<title>Homework 08</title>

<p>
Show all work and justify your reasoning whenever possible.
</p>

<exercise workspace="2 inches">
<title> Matrix Basics</title>

<p>
Consider the system
</p>

<me>
\mathbf{x}'=
\begin{bmatrix}
3 &amp;  -1\\
2 &amp;  0
\end{bmatrix}
\mathbf{x}.
</me>

<ol>
<li>Identify the coefficient matrix.</li>
<li>Find the trace.</li>
<li>Find the determinant.</li>
<li>Write the characteristic polynomial.</li>
<li>Without solving the system, predict whether the origin is stable or unstable. Why or why not?</li>
   <li>Now solve the system completely and verify your prediction.</li> 
</ol>

</exercise>


<exercise workspace="2 inches">
<title> Real Eigenvalues</title>

<p>
Consider
</p>

<me>
\mathbf{x}'=
\begin{bmatrix}
2 &amp;  1\\
1 &amp;  2
\end{bmatrix}
\mathbf{x}.
</me>

<ol>
<li>Find the characteristic polynomial.</li>
<li>Find the eigenvalues.</li>
<li>Find an eigenvector corresponding to each eigenvalue.</li>
<li>Without solving the system, predict whether the origin is stable or unstable. Why or why not?</li>
    <li> Now solve the system completely and verify your prediction. </li>
</ol>

</exercise>

<exercise workspace="2 inches">
<title> Complex Eigenvalues</title>

<p>
Consider
</p>

<me>
\mathbf{x}'=
\begin{bmatrix}
1 &amp;  -4\\
4 &amp;  1
\end{bmatrix}
\mathbf{x}.
</me>

<ol>
<li>Find the eigenvalues.</li>
<li>Identify α and β.</li>
<li>Will trajectories spiral inward or outward? Determine whether the origin is stable or unstable. Why?</li>
    <li> Now solve the system completely and verify your prediction. </li>
</ol>

</exercise>

<exercise workspace="2 inches">
<title> Interpreting Eigenvalues</title>

<p>
Match each eigenvalue pattern with the most appropriate classification.
</p>

<ol>
<li>λ₁ > 0, λ₂ > 0</li>
<li>λ₁ &lt; 0, λ₂ &lt; 0</li>
<li>One positive, one negative eigenvalue</li>
<li>α ± βi with α > 0</li>
<li>α ± βi with α &lt; 0</li>
<li>±βi</li>
</ol>

<p>
Possible classifications:
Node, Saddle, Spiral Sink, Spiral Source, Center.
</p>

</exercise>

<exercise workspace="2 inches">
<title> Three-Dimensional Systems</title>

<p>
Consider
</p>

<me>
\mathbf{x}'=
\begin{bmatrix}
6 &amp; -3 &amp; -4 \\
4 &amp; -1 &amp; -4 \\
3 &amp; -3 &amp; -1
\end{bmatrix}
\mathbf{x}.
</me>

<ol>
<li>Find the eigenvalues.</li>
<li>Which eigenvalue dominates long-term behavior?</li>
<li>What happens as t→∞?</li>
<li>Is the origin stable or unstable?</li>
<li>Now solve the system completely and verify your prediction.</li>
</ol>

</exercise>

<exercise workspace="1 inch">
<title> Basic Laplace Transforms</title>

<p>
Find the Laplace transform of each function.
</p>

<ol>
<li><m>f(t)=4</m></li>
<li><m>f(t)=e^{3t}</m></li>
<li><m>f(t)=\sin(5t)</m></li>
<li><m>f(t)=\cos(2t)</m></li>
<li><m>f(t)=t</m></li>
<li><m>f(t)=t^2</m></li>
</ol>

</exercise>

<exercise workspace="2 inches">
<title> Inverse Laplace Transforms</title>

<p>
Find the inverse Laplace transform of each expression.
</p>

<ol>
<li><m>\frac{1}{(s^2+4)(s+4)}</m></li>
<li><m>\frac{6+2s}{s^2+36}</m></li>
<li><m>\frac{s}{(s-1)^2+25}</m></li>
<li><m>\frac{1}{s^2(s+1)}</m></li>
<li><m>\frac{2}{(s^2-5s+6). }</m></li>
</ol>

</exercise>


<exercise workspace="2 inches">
<title> Shifting Theorem</title>

<p>
Find the inverse Laplace transform.
</p>

<ol>
<li><m>\frac{e^{-2s}}{s}</m></li>
<li><m>\frac{e^{-4s}}{s^2}</m></li>
<li><m>\frac{5e^{-3s}}{s^2+25}</m></li>
</ol>

<p>
Express answers using unit-step notation.
</p>

</exercise>

<exercise workspace="2 inches">
<title> Initial Value Problems</title>

<p>
Solve using Laplace transforms.
</p>
<me>
1.\; y'+3y=0,
\qquad
y(0)=4
</me>

<me>
2.\; y''+y=0,
\qquad
y(0)=1,
\qquad
y'(0)=0
</me>


</exercise>

<exercise workspace="2 inches">
<title> Error Analysis</title>

<p>
For each statement:
</p>

<ol>
<li>Identify whether it is correct or incorrect.</li>
<li>Explain the mistake.</li>
<li>Give the correct statement.</li>
</ol>

<p>
(A)
</p>

<me>
\mathcal{L}\{e^{-2t}\}
=
\frac{1}{s-2}
</me>

<p>
(B)
</p>

<me>
\mathcal{L}^{-1}
\left\{
\frac{e^{-3s}}s
\right\}
=
(t-3)e^{3t}
</me>

<p>
(C)
</p>

<me>
\mathcal{L}\{y'\}=sY
</me>

<p>
when <m>y(0)=5.</m>
</p>

</exercise>

<exercise workspace="2 inches">
<title>Matching Game</title>

<p>
Match each function to its Laplace transform.
</p>

<tabular>
<row>
<cell>1</cell>
<cell><m>e^{-t}</m></cell>
</row>
<row>
<cell>2</cell>
<cell><m>\sin(4t)</m></cell>
</row>
<row>
<cell>3</cell>
<cell><m>\cos(3t)</m></cell>
</row>
<row>
<cell>4</cell>
<cell><m>1</m></cell>
</row>
</tabular>

<p>
Transforms:
</p>

<ol>
<li><m>s/(s^2+9)</m></li>
<li><m>1/(s+1)</m></li>
<li><m>1/s</m></li>
<li><m>4/(s^2+16)</m></li>
</ol>

</exercise>

<exercise workspace="2 inches">
<title>Modeling Fantasy</title>

<p>
Describe a real-world situation that could be modeled by:
</p>

<ol>
<li>A system of differential equations.</li>
<li>An exponential decay function.</li>
<li>A delayed (shifted) input represented by a unit-step function.</li>
</ol>

<p>
Explain why the model is appropriate.
</p>

</exercise>

<subsection>
<title>MATLAB Reference: Systems of Differential Equations and Laplace Transforms</title>

<p>
The following MATLAB commands may be useful when exploring systems of differential equations,
phase portraits, eigenvalues, eigenvectors, Laplace transforms, and inverse Laplace transforms.
You are encouraged to modify the examples and investigate how changing parameters affects the results.
</p>

<subsubsection>
<title>Complete Systems Analysis Template</title>

<p>
Replace the matrix <m>A</m> with the system from a homework problem.
</p>

<cd>
clear
clc

A = [-5  2;
     -2 -1];

[V,D] = eig(A);

disp('Eigenvalues')
disp(diag(D))

disp('Eigenvectors')
disp(V)

[x,y] = meshgrid(-10:0.5:10,-10:0.5:10);

u = A(1,1)*x + A(1,2)*y;
v = A(2,1)*x + A(2,2)*y;

figure
h = gobjects(1,6);
labels = cell(1,6);

h(1) = quiver(x,y,u,v);
labels{1} = 'Vector field';

hold on

f = @(t,X) A*X;

for k = -2:2
    idx = k + 4;   % 2 to 6
    [t,X] = ode45(f,[0 5],[k+1;k-1]);
    h(idx) = plot(X(:,1),X(:,2),'LineWidth',2);
    labels{idx} = sprintf('IC [%d; %d]',k+1,k-1);
end

grid on
axis equal
xlim([-10 10])
ylim([-10 10])

legend(h,labels,'Location','best')
title('Phase Portrait')
xlabel('x')
ylabel('y')
</cd>

</subsubsection>

<subsubsection>
<title>Computing Laplace Transforms</title>

<p>
Define symbolic variables and compute a Laplace transform.
</p>

<cd>
syms t s

f = exp(-2*t);

F = laplace(f,t,s)

pretty(F)
</cd>

<p>
Expected output:
</p>

<me>
F(s) = \frac{1}{s+2}.
</me>

</subsubsection>
<subsubsection>
<title>Inverse Laplace Transforms</title>

<cd>
syms s t

F = 1/(s+3);

f = ilaplace(F,s,t)

pretty(f)
</cd>

<p>
Expected output:
</p>

<me>
e^{-3t}.
</me>

</subsubsection>

<subsubsection>
<title>Shifting Theorem and Unit Step Functions</title>

<p>
Delayed step function:
</p>

<cd>
syms s t

F = exp(-2*s)/s;

f = ilaplace(F,s,t)

pretty(f)
</cd>

<p>
Expected output (Heaviside step function):
</p>

<me>
u(t-2).
</me>

<p>
Delayed ramp:
</p>

<cd>
syms s t

F = exp(-3*s)/s^2;

ilaplace(F,s,t)
</cd>

<p>
Expected output:
</p>

<me>
(t-3)u(t-3).
</me>

</subsubsection>

<subsubsection>
<title>Solving Initial Value Problems</title>

<p>
Solve
</p>

<me>
y' + 2y = 0,
\qquad
y(0)=3.
</me>

<cd>
syms y(t)

ode = diff(y,t) + 2*y == 0;

cond = y(0) == 3;

sol = dsolve(ode,cond)

pretty(sol)
</cd>

<p>
Expected output:
</p>

<me>
y(t)=3e^{-2t}.
</me>

</subsubsection>
</subsection>


</worksheet>
